      "outputTokens": 897,
      "costUsd": 0.008135,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended on a chessboard. One grain of rice on the first square, two on the second, then four, eight and sixteen, with running totals one, three, seven, fifteen and thirty-one. Look at the totals. Each one is one less than a power of two. Thirty-one is thirty-two minus one. If the pattern keeps going, the first ten squares hold two to the ten, minus one grains, which is one thousand and twenty-three. But a pattern isn't a reason. Why does it work, and does something like it work for every geometric sequence?"
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-02",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-f06/scene-02.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1094,
      "worstWordGap": {
