          "text": "minus",
          "start": 3.469,
          "end": 3.77
        },
        {
          "text": "upward",
          "start": 37.01800000000003,
          "end": 37.241714285714316
        }
      ],
      "seconds": 37.64,
      "peakFraction": 0.84759521484375,
      "fullScaleSamples": 0,
      "silencesAtLeast1_5s": [],
      "lowScoreWords": [],
      "successfulRequestCount": 1,
      "loggedRequestAttempts": 1,
      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 133,
      "outputTokens": 941,
      "costUsd": 0.008535500000000001,
      "pronunciationText": "Take x minus one, times x to the n minus one, plus x to the n minus two, and so on, down to x, plus one. Multiplying the bracket by x raises every power by one, so the top term becomes x to the n. Multiplying by minus one gives the same powers as before, all negative. Every power from x up to x to the n minus one appears once with a plus and once with a minus, so they cancel. What's left is x to the n, minus one. Nothing here depended on which whole number n is, so it's true for every whole number from one upward."
    },
